{%- doc -%}
  PDP rating.

  Stars, rating value, then the review count as a link that scrolls to the
  reviews section on the same page:

    ★★★★★ 4.9 (12 reviews)

  The stars and the value come from the theme's own product-rating snippet, so
  the drawing, the half-star rounding and the accessibility labels stay
  Prestige's. Only the count is added, as an anchor - the snippet is rendered
  once, not twice, because a second render would draw another star inside the
  brackets.

  Data source is product.metafields.reviews.rating and .rating_count, the same
  metafields the product cards read and Judge.me keeps in sync. No second
  rating system, nothing hardcoded: the count wording comes from the theme's
  own product.rating_count translation, so pluralisation and other languages
  follow the theme.

  Renders nothing when the product has no ratings, so a new product shows no
  empty stars and no "0 reviews" link.

  Usage - a "Liquid" block where the rating should appear:

    {% render 'pdp-rating' %}

  The target defaults to #customer-reviews. Pass another id if the reviews
  section ends up with a different one:

    {% render 'pdp-rating', target: '#judgeme_product_reviews' %}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign review_target = target | default: '#customer-reviews'
  assign rating_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value
-%}

{%- if product.metafields.reviews.rating.value != blank -%}
  <div class="pdp-rating">
    {%- render 'product-rating', product: product -%}

    {%- if rating_count > 0 -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        A plain in-page anchor rather than a script: the browser does the
        scrolling, it works before any JavaScript has run, and the theme's own
        scroll-behavior applies.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a class="pdp-rating__link" href="{{ review_target }}">
        ({{ 'product.rating_count' | t: count: rating_count }})
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
